{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>Let us make man.</b> After He gave to the waters the power to swarm, bringing forth living souls — and the utterance concerning them was ״ישרצו המים״ — and concerning the beast, ״תוצא הארץ״, He said concerning man, who is the choicest of species, ״נעשה אדם״. By way of the plain sense (peshat): נעשה אדם — 'I shall make man,' together with the earth mentioned above, which shall bring forth the body from its elements, just as it did with beast and animal, in the manner of what is written: ״וייצר ה׳ אלהים את האדם עפר מן האדמה״; and the Holy One, blessed be He, breathed into him the supernal soul, in the manner of what is written: ״ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים״. And the meaning of בצלמנו is form (תאר), in the manner of what is written (Daniel 3:19): ״וצלם אנפוהי אשתני״; and it is written (Psalms 39:7): ״אך בצלם יתהלך איש״ — its meaning is form (תאר). כדמותנו — the resemblance of form, for those who are near in one matter are called similar to one another. And the intent of the verse is that man should be made in such a manner that he would resemble the supernal beings and the lower beings: to the supernal beings in respect of form (תאר), and to the lower beings in respect of resemblance (דמות), in that his body literally resembles dust. Thus wrote the Rav Ramban, of blessed memory, in the name of the sage R. David Kimhi, of blessed memory. And they further explained the word נעשה in the plural as the way of the language with kings and the nobles of the earth, that an individual among them speaks in the plural — and it is a manner of exaltation and honor. And behold, Laban was a single individual and said (Genesis 29:27): ״ונתנה לך גם את זאת״; and it is written (Numbers 22:6): ״אולי אוכל נכה בו ואגרשנו״; and it is written (Daniel 2:36): ״ופשריה נאמר קדם מלכא״ — the way of the great to speak thus. And the Rabbi Ibn Ezra, in the name of some (י״א), said: נעשה אדם is a passive participle, like (Isaiah 26:18): ״ישועות בל נעשה ארץ״ — end of quote. And there is no need to enter into all this difficulty." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> נעשה אדם — when Moses came to write the Torah and reached the point of writing נעשה אדם, he said before Him: Master of the universe, You are giving an opening to heretics to err! The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: Moses, write! And whoever wishes to err — let him come and err." "<b>And by way of reason (sekhel).</b> נעשה אדם. Know that the philosophers hold that the influence flowing from the Creator, blessed be He — who is the cause of all — flows first upon each separate intellect alone, the first one, which is caused by the Maker, blessed be He, and that intellect moves the encompassing sphere. From it eight separate intellects flow forth, each moving one sphere, down to the sphere of the moon. And we find a saying of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, that indicates somewhat of this, namely what they said: the Holy One, blessed be He, does nothing without looking to the celestial retinue (פמליא של מעלה). And the explanation of the matter is that the Creator, blessed be He, performs all His actions by means of intermediaries. And we find also in the plain sense of the Torah support for this matter; and concerning this He said here נעשה אדם in the plural, to indicate the intermediaries. And likewise (Genesis 11:7): ״הבה נרדה ונבלה שם וגו׳״; and likewise it is written (Genesis 19:24): ״וה׳ המטיר על סדום ועל עמורה גפרית ואש״, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: wherever וה׳ is said, it means He and His court. And the meaning of מסתכל is that He gives power within them to govern lower beings, and it is He who grants them dominion on earth, in the manner of what is written (Job 38:33): ״אם תשים משטרו בארץ״; and here they said מסתכל, and in another place they said נמלך — the Holy One, blessed be He, does nothing without consulting the celestial retinue, as it is written (Daniel 4:14): ״בגזרת עירין פתגמא ובמאמר וגו׳״. And those appointed ones in whom He, blessed be He, consults are nothing but the instruments of His will, showing His action through them — like the soul, which shows its actions by means of the body; and it is known that the actions of the body are only by the power of the soul. And we find in the Torah one support for this matter, which is what is written (Amos 3:7): ״כי לא יעשה ה׳ אלהים דבר כי אם גלה סודו אל עבדיו הנביאים״ — and if to His servants the prophets He reveals His secret and makes known His actions, all the more so to the angels who pour forth power upon the prophets to inform them of the future. Yet even so, He, blessed be He, is in need of none of the existent beings besides Him; and all apart from Him are always in need of Him, blessed be He."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:27:42.094666611Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}